<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  org.ruyaos.Library1 — everything installed that can be started: Steam games (read from Steam's
  library files, read-only) and installed Flatpak apps, as one list.
  Served by ruya-library (T-112) at /org/ruyaos/Library1 on the system bus, as the system user
  ruya-library (T-163). It reads the system Flatpak installation itself and never a profile's home
  (ProtectHome=yes): Steam games come per profile from ruya-session (ADR-045, below). The service
  sends ItemsChanged by itself when apps are installed or removed and when the active profile's
  view changes. Launching is not part of this interface (the session service does it, from the
  item's "launch" string).

  Per-profile Steam items (ADR-045, RUY-1056). Steam libraries live in each profile's home.
  ruya-session runs `ruya-library profile-scan` as the profile's user (a short-lived, hardened unit
  in the profile's manager) and hands its stdout, unchanged, to PutProfileItems. ruya-library keeps
  one Steam list per profile and serves the view of ActiveProfile: ListItems is the Flatpak items
  plus the Steam items of ActiveProfile ("" = nobody signed in: Flatpak items only). ruya-session
  sets ActiveProfile with SetActiveProfile on every change of Profiles1.ActiveProfile, before any
  scan, and again when Library1's bus name gets a new owner (ruya-library may not talk to
  Profiles1). A put for a profile that is not active is kept and changes nothing visible; a change
  of the active profile that changes the merged list sends ItemsChanged.
  PutProfileItems and SetActiveProfile are root only (org.ruyaos.Caller "root"): the service
  answers "not_allowed" to any caller whose Unix uid is not 0, and the bus policy denies them to
  ruya-shell. In development (the session bus) the uid check is off, said once in the log.

  Profile items document, JSON v1 (PutProfileItems `data`; what profile-scan prints):
    {"version":1,"items":[{"appid":"<1-10 digits>","name":s,"last_played":x}, ...]}
  The data comes from a reader that runs as the profile's user, so ruya-library checks it again:
    - the whole document is refused ("invalid") when it is over 32 MiB, not JSON, not an object
      with "version" 1 and an "items" array, or holds more than 4096 items;
    - an item is dropped alone when its appid is not 1 to 10 ASCII digits, its name is missing or empty
      (after control characters are stripped and the text is trimmed), or its last_played is
      negative or not an integer (absent = 0);
    - a name is cut at 120 characters; a repeated appid keeps its first item;
    - "launch" is built by ruya-library ("steam://rungameid/<appid>"), never read from the document.
  Unknown keys are ignored, at both levels. Reserved for later versions of the reader, ignored by
  v1: "art" (an item's pictures, RUY-958) and "facts" (appinfo facts, RUY-1070).
  Contract between shell (Qt/QML) and services (Rust); see docs/specs/03-services-and-apps.md §5.

  Every record is an a{sv} dictionary, so fields can be added later without breaking clients
  (unknown keys are ignored).

  Item record (a{sv}), keys:
    id           s    "steam:<appid>" or "flatpak:<application id>"; stable
    kind         s    "game" or "app". Steam items are always "game". A Flatpak item is a "game" when
                      "Game" is in the Categories of the [Desktop Entry] group of its exported
                      desktop entry (system installation), else "app" (also without such a file)
    title        s    display name (a .play app: the name in play.json, English else the first language)
    source       s    "steam" or "flatpak"
    launch       s    opaque launch spec for the session service: "steam://rungameid/<appid>"
                      for Steam, "flatpak:<application id>" for Flatpak
    art          a{ss} artwork by role ("tile", "hero", "icon") -> file path or URL; may be empty.
                      A .play app (below) adds the roles "logo", "card", "cover", "preview" (a short
                      video) and "trailer" (a video); each is present only when the package has it
    last_played  x    unix seconds, 0 if unknown

  A .play app (RUY-852) is a Flatpak app "play.<id>" that ruya-play installed; ruya-library reads its
  extra keys from its deployment
  (/var/lib/flatpak/app/play.<id>/current/active/files/: play.json, share/ruyaos/play/art/ and
  share/ruyaos/play/trust.json). Nothing is verified here (the tier and signer are what ruya-play
  recorded at install). Only apps installed from the console's own Flatpak remote
  ("ruyaos-play-local", ruya_play_art::PLAY_REMOTE) are read: a "play.*" app from any other remote
  (".play" is a real top-level domain) is a plain item, so it cannot claim a tier. Text is cut at 120
  characters and genres at four. A deployment without a readable play.json is a plain item.

  Item record (a{sv}), extra keys of a .play app (non-breaking; every one is absent for any other item):
    play         b    true
    genres       as   play.json genres (up to four); may be empty
    players      (uu) fewest and most players; absent when play.json has none
    controller   s    "full", "partial" or "none"; absent when play.json has none
    age_rating   a{ss} OARS attribute -> "none", "mild", "moderate" or "intense"; absent when none
    play_tier    s    "official", "developer" or "unverified"; absent when trust.json is missing or
                      cannot be read
    play_signer  s    the signer's name (else its key id); absent with no tier or no signer
    play_signer_key_id s  the signer's key id as trust.json's signer_id holds it (32 hex, never
                      computed here); absent with no tier or no signer
    play_screenshots as  the 1080p screenshots in order (paths the shell can read, at most 20);
                      absent when there are none
    play_runner  s    the runner the launch would use for the game's run.component, "<family> <version>"
                      ("wine 10.2": the family has no space, the version may); absent for a native
                      game and when play_resolve_error is present
    play_runner_source s  "bundled" (the copy in the package) or "system" (the image's runner,
                      /usr/lib/ruyaos/play/runners); present with play_runner
    play_runner_tested s  the version the developer tested the component with (play.json
                      "tested"); absent when the package names none or play_runner is absent
    play_resolve_error s  one English sentence saying why the launch would refuse (no runner of the
                      family in the image, the image's runner older than the package needs, or the
                      package's files cannot be read); absent when the game can start or is native.
                      For developers and logs: a screen shows its own sentence for play_resolve_code
    play_resolve_code s  the stable code of that refusal (RUY-905): a resolve.* code of 05 section
                      4.5 (resolve.no_system_runner, resolve.system_too_old, resolve.system_too_new,
                      resolve.no_bundled), a launch.* code of the launcher (launch.runner_missing,
                      launch.runner_kind, launch.damaged) or the format's problem code for damaged
                      files; present with play_resolve_error, absent otherwise
    play_fallback b   true: the choice asked for cannot be honoured (no bundled copy, or no usable
                      image runner of the bundled copy's family), so play_runner is the package
                      default's runner (RUY-905); only with play_runner, absent otherwise. ListItems
                      resolves the package default, which always can be honoured: absent there
                      These six are the launcher's own resolution (ruya-play-launch, ADR-037)
                      run against the deployment and the image's runners, the package's default
                      choice; they say nothing of a per-game choice (ResolveRunner below)

  Order: last_played descending, then title (case-insensitive), then id.

  Runners (RUY-959). The image's runners are the folders of /usr/lib/ruyaos/play/runners that the
  launcher itself would accept (ruya_play_launch::runners::scan_in, ADR-038): a folder whose
  runner.json is missing or invalid is left out, with a line in the journal. Order: folder name.

  Runner record (a{sv}), keys:
    family       s    runner.json family ("proton-ge", "wine")
    kind         s    "wine", "proton", "emulator" or "engine"
    version      s    runner.json version ("GE-Proton11-13", "10.0")
    licence      s    its SPDX licence; absent when runner.json names none
    source_url   s    where its source is (https); absent when runner.json names none

  Runner choice (RUY-959, 05 section 6.2). Each profile keeps one choice per .play game:
    "package"    what the package says (the default; storing it removes the profile's entry)
    "bundled"    the copy in the package
    "system"     the image's runner
  exactly the values ruya-play-launch takes as its runner argument (ADR-037). The choices are stored per profile
  under /var/lib/ruyaos/library (one file per profile); `profile` is a Profiles1 id, which the caller
  (the shell for the signed-in profile, ruya-session for the profile it launches for) passes. `id` is
  a .play item's id ("flatpak:play.<id>").

  Status strings (stable API):
    "ok"         done
    "unknown"    no item with that id is installed
    "not_play"   the item is not a .play app (a Steam game, a plain Flatpak app, a play.* app from
                 another remote)
    "invalid"    a malformed profile id or a choice that is not one of the three; for
                 PutProfileItems and SetActiveProfile a malformed profile id or a refused document
    "failed"     the choice could not be stored
    "not_allowed" PutProfileItems or SetActiveProfile from a caller whose uid is not 0

  ResolveRunner's answer holds the six runner keys of the item record, for the choice asked for
  instead of the package's default: the launcher's own resolution with that runner argument
  (ruya_play_launch::dry_run, the same code ListItems' keys come from).

  Resolution record (a{sv}), keys (every one may be absent):
    play_runner  s    "<family> <version>"; absent for a native game and when play_resolve_error is
                      present
    play_runner_source s  "bundled" or "system"; present with play_runner
    play_runner_tested s  the version the developer tested the component with; absent when none
    play_resolve_error s  one English sentence saying why the launch would refuse with that choice
    play_resolve_code s  the stable code of that refusal (as in the item record); present with
                      play_resolve_error
    play_fallback b   true: that choice cannot be honoured and play_runner is the package
                      default's runner (as in the item record); only with play_runner
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.ruyaos.Library1">

    <!-- The whole library, in the order above. -->
    <method name="ListItems">
      <arg name="items" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Read the Flatpak list again and ask for the active profile's Steam items again
         (ProfileScanWanted, when a profile is active). Returns after the Flatpak scan;
         ItemsChanged follows if the list differs from before. -->
    <method name="Rescan"/>

    <!-- The list changed; call ListItems again. -->
    <signal name="ItemsChanged"/>

    <!-- Root only (ruya-session): the Steam items of `profile`, a JSON v1 document (above). The
         profile's list is replaced by the document's items; ItemsChanged follows when `profile`
         is ActiveProfile and the list differs. "ok", "invalid" (a malformed profile id or a
         refused document: the profile's list is left as it was) or "not_allowed". -->
    <method name="PutProfileItems">
      <annotation name="org.ruyaos.Caller" value="root"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="data" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Root only (ruya-session): the signed-in profile, "" for nobody. ActiveProfile changes
         (PropertiesChanged) and ItemsChanged follows when the merged list differs. "ok",
         "invalid" (not "" and not a profile id) or "not_allowed". -->
    <method name="SetActiveProfile">
      <annotation name="org.ruyaos.Caller" value="root"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Whose view ListItems serves: the last SetActiveProfile, "" at start (nobody, until
         ruya-session says). The shell hides Steam items while it is not the signed-in profile. -->
    <property name="ActiveProfile" type="s" access="read">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Property.EmitsChangedSignal" value="true"/>
    </property>

    <!-- Rescan asks for `profile`'s Steam items again: ruya-session runs the profile's reader
         and puts the result. -->
    <signal name="ProfileScanWanted">
      <arg name="profile" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- The image's runners, in the order above. -->
    <method name="ListRunners">
      <arg name="runners" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- The profile's runner choice for a .play game: "package" when it has none. `choice` is ""
         unless the status is "ok". -->
    <method name="GetRunnerChoice">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="choice" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Stores the profile's runner choice for a .play game ("package" removes it).
         RunnerChoiceChanged follows when the choice differs from before. -->
    <method name="SetRunnerChoice">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="choice" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- What the launch of a .play game would run with that choice (a Resolution record, empty
         unless the status is "ok"). Nothing is stored. -->
    <method name="ResolveRunner">
      <arg name="id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="choice" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="resolution" type="a{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- A profile's choice for a game changed (SetRunnerChoice). -->
    <signal name="RunnerChoiceChanged">
      <arg name="profile" type="s"/>
      <arg name="id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="choice" type="s"/>
    </signal>

  </interface>
</node>
